---
source: crates/dlin/tests/integration/cli.rs
expression: long_help(args)
---
Compute downstream impact analysis for one or more models.
Exact unique IDs take precedence. Short names prefer models. Use a unique ID to resolve ambiguity.

Reports downstream dependents of the selected node types and assigns severity levels:
  Critical  impacts exposures (dashboards, reports)
  High      impacts table/incremental materializations or mart models
  Medium    impacts staging or intermediate models
  Low       impacts tests only

Text output: human-readable report with severity and distance (degree).
JSON output: structured array of impact reports for CI/programmatic use.
Affected counts for unselected types are null in JSON and shown as out of
scope in text. Exposure paths are reported only when exposures are selected.

Completeness:
  impacted_nodes are the selected nodes reached through the edges dlin found. They
  may include nodes reached through an edge that is only a guess, and they
  can miss nodes whose dependencies dlin could not analyse.
  exploration says whether the result can miss a selected node:
    complete    no gap that could hide a selected node
    incomplete  gaps lists why it may: unresolved dependencies on a path
                to an unreached target, an input skipped while reading
                the project (input_skipped), or a scope not assessed at
                all (not_assessed, e.g. manifest_dependencies: manifest
                mode takes the manifest's dependencies as they are)
  An input that is an unresolved reference has an empty exploration.
  source_node and each impacted node carry their own dependency_analysis.
  A proof of no impact applies only to the node_types in the report. It
  requires an empty impacted_nodes list and a complete exploration.
  The exit code does not depend on completeness.

Stdin/pipe support:
  Accepts model names or file paths on stdin (one per line). File paths (detected by extension or path separator) are resolved to model names using the dbt project configuration.

Exit codes:
  0   Success (impact computed, even if no downstream dependents)
  1   Error (no models specified, or all specified models not found)

Usage: dlin impact [OPTIONS] [MODEL]...

Arguments:
  [MODEL]...
          Model names to analyze impact for (also accepts stdin)

Options:
      --error-format <ERROR_FORMAT>
          Error/warning output format on stderr: text (default) or json.
          
          When json, each diagnostic is one JSON object on its own line. Every
          object has {"level":"error"|"warning"|"info","what":"...","why":...,"hint":...},
          where why and hint are either strings or null. Typed diagnostics add a
          "code" and fields of their own; for example dependency_analysis_summary
          adds counts by reason type and namespace. Read code and the typed fields,
          not the text of what, why and hint.

          Possible values:
          - text: Human-readable error messages (default)
          - json: Structured JSON on stderr: {"level":"...","what":"...","why":...,"hint":...}
          
          [env: DLIN_ERROR_FORMAT=]
          [default: text]

  -p, --project-dir <PROJECT_DIR>
          Path to dbt project directory
          
          [default: .]

      --cache-dir <CACHE_DIR>
          Directory for caching manifest analysis results (default: <project-dir>/.dlin_cache). Has no effect with --source sql, which has no cache
          
          [env: DLIN_CACHE_DIR=]

      --verbose
          Show the details of the model dependency analysis on stderr.
          
          By default, a command that builds the lineage graph prints at most one
          configuration warning and one dependency_analysis_summary diagnostic, which
          counts the reasons of the whole project by type and namespace. With
          --verbose it also prints one diagnostic per reason (with the node and the
          call site) and per skipped input, and the info diagnostics for default
          paths that do not exist. It changes no analysis. --quiet takes precedence.

      --no-cache
          Disable the manifest analysis cache (always re-parse the manifest, results are not saved). Has no effect with --source sql, which has no cache
          
          [env: DLIN_NO_CACHE=]

      --refresh-cache
          Discard the existing manifest analysis cache and rebuild it. Has no effect with --source sql, which has no cache
          
          [env: DLIN_REFRESH_CACHE=]

  -o, --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format: text (default) or json
          
          [default: text]
          [possible values: text, json]

      --node-type <NODE_TYPES>
          Select downstream node types (comma-separated). Default: all except test.
          Available types: model, source, seed, snapshot, test, exposure, semantic_model, metric, saved_query.

      --source <SOURCE>
          Data source for building the lineage graph.
          
            sql (default)   Parse SQL files directly — no dbt or Python required.
                            Exposures and generic tests are detected from YAML with
                            dlin-specific IDs; use manifest mode for exact test
                            dependency resolution.
            manifest        Use dbt manifest.json for full accuracy. Requires
                            prior `dbt compile` (or `dbt run`/`dbt build`).

          Possible values:
          - sql:      Parse SQL files directly — no dbt/Python required. Exposures and generic tests are detected from YAML with dlin-specific IDs; use manifest mode for exact test dependency resolution
          - manifest: Use dbt manifest.json — full accuracy, requires prior `dbt compile`
          
          [default: sql]

      --manifest-path <MANIFEST_PATH>
          Path to manifest.json file or directory containing target/manifest.json.
          
          Default: <project-dir>/target/manifest.json

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress warning messages

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

Examples:
  # Text report for a single model
  dlin impact orders

  # JSON output for CI integration
  dlin impact orders -o json

  # Report downstream models and tests
  dlin impact orders --node-type model,test

  # Multiple models at once
  dlin impact orders stg_orders -o json

  # From git diff (pipe changed files)
  git diff --name-only main | dlin impact -o json

  # Use manifest instead of SQL parsing
  dlin impact orders --source manifest --manifest-path target/manifest.json
